{"id":"confetti_pop","compound_name":"Graphs::Particles::confetti_pop","source_path":"confetti_pop/confetti_pop.json","category":"Particles","author":"Autodesk","description":"Combines aero and particle systems to create a confetti-popping-out-of-a-bottle effect","documentation_file":"confetti_pop_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Confetti Pop Graph\n\n### Description\n\nThis graph combines the aero and particle simulation systems to create a confetti pop effect.\n\nFirst the graph creates an emitter, in this case a mesh sphere, which is used as source for both aero and particles.  The idea is to simulate a mass of air with intense `initial_velocity`, have the particles trapped in that mass of air momentarily, and then allow them to fall under the force of their gravity.  This is why it's important to emit particles and aero at the same time, from the same geometry.  \n\nAs the sphere emits particles and air, that same air simulation is used as an input for a volume_influence field plugged only into the particle solver.  This is what ensures that the particles will move with the same velocities as the aero, binding them together.\n\nAfter a certain point, when you no longer want the particles trapped in the air, you can simply turn off the aero solver, and the particle simulation will take over, allowing gravity to make them fall and collide with a ground plane.  \n\nFinally, you want the confetti at rest to lay flat on the ground, and no longer feel influences like turbulence.  To do this, a clever setup of influences, modulators and modifiers has been added.  \n\nAll of this is highlighted with backdrops in the graph.  \n\n### Inputs, Controls & Outputs\n\nThe inputs for this graph are: an implicit sphere serving as the source for both the aero and particle simulations, and an implicit plane serving as the ground for collisions only for the particles.\n\nThe output is the resulting particle simulation point cloud. \n\n### Best Practices and Tips\n\n- Since the aero simulation is only used as an influence in this example, it need not be very high resolution.  However, it does require a high `initial velocity` to create the initial \"pop\", which creates a bit of a slowdown. \n\n- The aero simulation can also be cached and read back in as VDB, to be plugged in the volume_influence.  \n\n- Although the particles have gravity, the influence of the aero is stronger and so this only takes over after the aero simulation is turned off.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","particles":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":48,"connections":53,"values":176,"compounds":2},"node_types":{"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":6,"Simulation::Influence::modulate_influence":4,"Simulation::Particles::set_spin_value":3,"Simulation::Particles::set_orientation_value":3,"Value::Math::float3":3,"Simulation::Influence::drag_influence":2,"Simulation::Influence::modifier_influence":2,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":2,"Rendering::Materials::standard_surface_mat":2,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Aero::source_air":1,"Simulation::Particles::particle_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Particles::simulate_particles":1,"Simulation::Particles::source_particles":1,"Simulation::Influence::volume_influence":1,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":1,"Core::Time::time":1,"Core::Type_Conversion::to_bool":1,"Core::Object::set_property":1,"Simulation::Influence::turbulence_influence":1,"Simulation::Common::collider":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_sphere":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_plane":1,"assign_gray_shader":1,"Simulation::Particles::source_particle_rotations":1,"Core::Graph::